sections.body:
A new laptop joins the office network. Before it can open a single web page it needs four settings:

- an **IP address**, so other devices can reach it;
- a **subnet mask**, so it knows which destinations are on its own subnet;
- a **default gateway**, the router it sends everything else to;
- one or more **DNS server** addresses, so it can turn names into addresses.

You could type these into every device by hand, and for routers, switches, servers and printers you usually do, because their addresses should never change. For hundreds of laptops and phones that come and go, manual setup means typos, duplicate addresses and no easy way to change the DNS server for everyone. **DHCP (Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol)** centralizes the job. A DHCP server holds the settings for each subnet and hands them out on request.

What the client receives is a **lease**: the address is loaned for a set time (one day is the default on a Cisco router). Halfway through, the client quietly asks to renew and usually keeps the same address. If the device leaves and the lease runs out, the address returns to the pool for someone else. That is why DHCP scales: addresses are handed out on demand and reclaimed automatically.

DHCP runs over UDP. Servers listen on **port 67** and clients on **port 68**. The server is **stateful**: it remembers which client holds which address.

Servers can allocate addresses three ways. **Dynamic** allocation, the normal case, leases from a pool for a limited time. **Automatic** allocation gives an infinite lease. **Static** (manual) allocation, often called a reservation, always gives the same address to one specific client, identified by its MAC address or client ID, which suits printers.

When DHCP fails completely, Windows and macOS clients give themselves an address from **169.254.0.0/16** (APIPA, a link-local address). A host showing 169.254.x.x is the classic sign that it never heard back from a DHCP server.
